W={
'1':"Chapter 10's Bridge ends on prudence as \"the virtue that teaches us *how* to love, so that truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are lived as one act toward the real person in front of us.\" The opening now picks that up (\"between the definition and the person in front of you\") without re-running the Bridge. **Your 7 October ruling:** every part of the definition is a *facet* of one love, like one diamond seen from every side, and truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are facets too, not a separate class. \"Thread by thread\" and the three \"pillars\" go. \"The four facets\" goes too, because it suggests love has only four: where the chapter means those four it now names them (truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice), and where it means the whole it says \"the facets of love.\" The other chapters are queued as their own todo (memory.html Future Task 167). \"Proved\" → \"showed\" is the Chapter 2 ruling (todo 43 flagged this exact line). The sin definition now lets the Church speak: CCC 1849 calls sin \"failure in genuine love for God and neighbor.\" The old wording (\"a perceived good for ourselves at the expense of… another\") left out God and sins against oneself.",
'2':"Whole verses or \"cf.\", checked against the RSVCE (bible-history.com) and the plain RSV (biblestudytools.com); every verse agreed in both. Fixed: Wisdom 8:7 epigraph (was \"For [wisdom] teaches…\"); Luke 15:20 and 15:24 now whole; \"he came to himself\" and \"while he was yet at a distance\" become plain words with cf.; Genesis 1:27, Isaiah 11:2 and 1 Kings 3:9 whole (1 Kings had an ellipsis and Isaiah had \"Spirit\" capitalized where the RSV has \"spirit\"); 2 Cor 5:17 and Gal 2:20 as cf.; the James 1:22 and Proverbs 3:5–6 fragments that echoed the full verses become plain prose. Wisdom 9:13–17 had an ellipsis: verse 13 is now quoted in the sentence, and the blockquote is verse 17 alone, the answer. Luke 23:42–43 is cited, not quoted, so your printing's \"into your kingdom\" is not at stake here.",
'3':"Where the Church has already said it, she says it. CCC 1806 supplies \"right reason in action\" (its translation, with \"following Aristotle\"), the warning against timidity, and why prudence is called *auriga virtutum*; CCC 2278–2279 now carry the end-of-life case, including that ordinary care can never be stopped (the old text said nothing about that) and the double-effect sentence in the Catechism's own words. **CCC 982 was misquoted:** it says \"the gates of *forgiveness* should always be open,\" not \"the gates of conversion should always stand open.\" Aquinas references added: three acts with command the chief (II-II q.47 a.8), the order of charity (q.26), *gnome* for exceptional cases (q.51 a.4), counsel perfecting prudence (q.52 a.2). Veritatis Splendor is now cited by section (79–81).",
'4':"**The cellar.** Two corrections. (a) \"Offer your own life\" could be read as killing yourself to silence your own cry; the Church allows laying down one's life (giving yourself up) but never taking an innocent life, one's own included, so it now says that. (b) Hushing the child is not wrong; what is forbidden is holding on until he is dead, \"choosing his death as the means.\" That keeps the parents from appearing to choose any death, and it is the same intention test CCC 2279 uses. **\"No good is greater than the single soul\"** (twice) overclaims, since God is the greatest good; the point is that a person is never a means. **Line 67:** \"we are not asked to be\" as good as God sat badly with Matt 5:48, which Chapter 10 now treats as the call to holiness. **The child who asks:** CCC 2358 asks for \"respect, compassion, and sensitivity,\" so the case now says love is not approval and not contempt either. **Absolution** comes through the Sacrament of Confession, so it is named.",
'5':"Wisdom 8:7 puts self-control first, not prudence, so \"with prudence at its head\" was not true. \"Before a word of the New Testament was written\" depends on a date scholars dispute, so it is gone. \"Volumes later cut from some Bibles\" reads as a jab, so the clause is removed. The Book of Wisdom is written in Solomon's voice; the Church does not teach that Solomon wrote it, so \"Solomon… set the reason down\" now reads \"written in Solomon's voice.\" \"The first saint canonized by Christ Himself\" is popular piety, not the Church's language; the Gospel fact (promised Paradise that day) stays. \"Dozens of questions\" → ten (II-II qq.47–56). Also softened: \"the worst thing a person can do,\" \"a growing number of countries,\" \"in that culture, was as much as to say.\"",
'6':"\"Unearned\" (twice) is in the banned family. Hand imagery (todo 129): \"love cannot be forced into a closed hand\" and the Examen's \"Where did I keep my hand closed?\" both go (the second is in group 8). Five sentences began with \"So.\"",
'7':"Checked against the chapters themselves. Covenant (\"I give because you are\") is Chapter 4 (ch. 4 line 196), not the chapter on sacrifice. The order of love (ch. 9 line 295, which promises this chapter) and the abuse case (ch. 9 lines 319–321) are Chapter 9. \"Without God… mere sentiment\" is Chapter 3 (line 189 says \"sentiment,\" not \"preference\"). Sin is Chapter 14. The freedom quotation is already in Chapter 4 (line 71), so here it is a callback that names its source; verified at p. 135 of *Love and Responsibility* (Willetts), ch. II.",
'8':"§12: Binding Check, \"When the Thread Is Cut\" and the opening opposing sin are required in Chapters 6–9 only, and Chapter 10 was ruled exempt, so nothing is added. The Bridge is required in every chapter and must name the next chapter by number; Chapter 11 had none. The last paragraph becomes \"The Bridge to the Lawgiver\" and names Chapter 12 (\"The Lawgiver Is Love\"). \"Prayer Practice\" is on §12's removed-from-template list, so the Examen moves into the body as prose, ahead of the Bridge, and keeps the father/two-sons question. Only one --- divider remains before Further Study.",
'9':"Todo 127. In the body: Aquinas and Augustine as Doctors of the Church at first mention; Francis de Sales and Thérèse noted as Doctors; St. Ignatius, founder of the Jesuits; Thomas à Kempis was listed among \"the saints\" but is not canonized, so he is named as a priest. Further Study follows Chapter 10's form (dates, office, then what to read); Augustine gains *City of God* XV.22; Aristotle moves to the end of the thinkers list with \"Greek philosopher before Christ\" (no credit for the definition of love). Fixed the 1 Kings 3:9 and Wisdom 7:7 quotations; Isaiah 11:2 does not list seven gifts in the RSV, so its line changes. Added CCC 1849–1851, 2276–2279, 2357–2359 and *Dignitas Infinita*.",
}
